# Line endings, declared instead of inherited.
#
# Why this file exists: the Git for Windows *system* config
# (C:\Program Files\Git\etc\gitconfig) sets core.autocrlf=true and neither this repo nor
# the user config overrides it, so every clone here decides stored form per file by
# heuristic and rewrites the working copy on checkout. The symptom shows up on ordinary
# commits: `git add STATUS-2026-09-24.md` printed
#     warning: in the working copy of 'STATUS-2026-09-24.md', LF will be replaced by
#     CRLF the next time Git touches it
# which is the repo saying it does not know what form this file is supposed to be in.
#
# What this repo's state actually is, measured rather than assumed
# (`git ls-files --eol`, 2026-09-28, 246 tracked files):
#     index:  243 LF, 2 binary/none, 0 CRLF, 0 mixed
#     work:   105 CRLF, 109 LF, 29 mixed
# The stored form is already uniform. The CRLF is added at *checkout* by autocrlf and
# normalised away again at *add*, so it costs nothing today and everything the first
# time a tool compares bytes instead of content. Unlike Memex_Core, this repo needs no
# renormalisation commit: there is nothing to renormalise, which was checked rather than
# inferred - `git add --renormalize .` over all 246 tracked files staged exactly nothing.
# These rules keep that true, and make the answer identical on a macOS or Linux clone
# (plan C3 wants both, and a dmg cannot be built from this box).
#
# Scope note: `text=auto` and the rules below do NOT rewrite existing blobs, and here
# they do not need to. Nothing has to be regenerated for this to take effect; files
# already CRLF on disk stay CRLF until they are re-checked out, and `git status` stays
# clean either way because the same rules apply in both directions.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Source and content this app ships. Named explicitly so the heuristic is never what
# decides: the .py files in the repo root are CAD scratch that happens to live here,
# and they are text like everything else in this list.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.jsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.scad text eol=lf

# NSIS installer script (build/*.nsh). Text, and LF is read fine by makensis.
*.nsh text eol=lf

# cmd.exe parses batch files line by line and LF can misbehave interactively, so these
# keep CRLF on checkout while still storing LF. PowerShell reads LF happily, so *.ps1
# stays on the default above.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary is a correctness rule, not a style rule: a "normalised" binary is corrupt and
# the damage is silent. Only one image is tracked today (public/icon.png); the rest of
# this list covers what lives in this directory untracked - the sink/faucet CAD scratch
# (*.stl, *.png, *.scad renders) and the packaged output under release*/ (*.exe,
# *.blockmap, *.zip). Those paths are gitignored, so this is a guard against a forced
# `git add -f` and against a future asset landing outside the ignore patterns. It is
# deliberately not left to the auto heuristic, which has mistaken model weights and
# dumps for text before.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.ogg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.stl binary
*.3mf binary
*.gcode binary
*.glb binary
*.gltf binary
*.obj binary
*.fbx binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.tar binary
*.7z binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.lib binary
*.pdb binary
*.blockmap binary
*.appx binary
*.dmg binary
*.pyc binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary

# Git LFS is configured on this box (filter "lfs" in the system config) but nothing here
# declares `filter=lfs`, so no file is in LFS and none is pointer-rewritten. If packaged
# artifacts ever need to be committed rather than ignored, that is an LFS decision made
# on purpose - not something to discover in a rejected push.
